README for corelib::progs/eval/bivariateRange

Dec 2025:
	This folder contains all the code and data reported in our paper,
		"Range Functions of Any Convergence Order and
					their Amortized Complexity Analysis"
			by
		Kai Hormann, Chee Yap, Thomas Chen and Bingwei Zhang

	 (paper was submitted to GMP 2026)

*A subfolder "data" contains several families of square-free real
    polynomials (Chebyshev, Hermite, Mignotte, Wilkinson, and sparse
    polynomials) of various degrees.

* We implemented the EVAL algorithm for isolating the real roots of a
    polynomial f(x) over a given interval [a,b].

* There are 12 variants of EVAL depending on the range function used to
    evaluate f(x) and f'(x):

        evalH4          -- Hermite interpolation, order 4
        evalH4cheap     -- cheap version of Hermite order 4
        evalH4ncheap    -- non-maximal level cheap Hermite order 4
        evalL3          -- Lagrange interpolation, order 3
        evalL3cheap     -- cheap version of Lagrange order 3
        evalL3ncheap    -- non-maximal level cheap Lagrange order 3
        evalL4          -- Lagrange interpolation, order 4
        evalL4cheap     -- cheap version of Lagrange order 4
        evalT2          -- Taylor interpolation, order 2
        evalT2min       -- minimal level Taylor order 2
        evalT3          -- Taylor interpolation, order 3
        evalT4          -- Taylor interpolation, order 4

* For the experiments we consider several benchmark algebraic curves
    (named Clover4, Clover5, Clover8, and Grass in the code).  
    For each polynomial we evaluate the range of f(x,y) over a box
    B = [-1.2,1.2] × [-1.2,1.2], which is uniformly subdivided into
    1024 subboxes.

* For each subbox we compute the range enclosure produced by the
    chosen range function.  The tightness of the enclosure is measured
    by the width of the resulting interval.

* The total width over all subboxes is accumulated and the
    average enclosure width is reported as

        efficacy = total_width / number_of_boxes

    which provides a measure of how tight the computed ranges are.

* Running time is measured using BenchmarkTools with repeated runs.
    For each experiment we report

        - minimum running time
        - median running time
        - mean running time
        - memory usage

* The experiments produce several tables and figures:

        Table 1: sizes of the subdivision trees for different EVAL methods

        Table 2: average running time using 1024-bit floating-point
                 arithmetic

        Table 3: average running time using multi-precision rational
                 arithmetic

        Figure 1: speedup ratio of cheap Hermite order 4 versus
                  cheap Lagrange order 3 for different polynomial
                  families (degrees on the x-axis)

        Figure 2: speedup ratio of the non-maximal level cheap
                  Lagrange order 3 versus the maximal level.
                  The level parameter varies along the x-axis.

* A Makefile provides the targets

        table1, table2, table3, figure1, figure2

    to automatically reproduce all experimental results.

